diagnosis_text:
The user-reported loss of connectivity between pc1 (195.11.14.2) and pc2 (200.1.1.2) is **not confirmed** by any live evidence. A full SADE broad-search pass (Phases A through D) found no fault on any device or path in the topology.

**Affected component:** None identified. The WAN link between router1 and router2, specifically suspected by the NOC, is fully operational.

**Mechanism:** Live end-to-end pings from pc1 to pc2 and from pc2 to pc1 both complete with 0% packet loss and sub-millisecond RTT. The BGP sessions on router1 (ASN 1, advertising 195.11.14.0/24) and router2 (ASN 2, advertising 200.1.1.0/24) are both in the **Established** state. All FRR daemons (watchfrr, zebra, bgpd) are running on both routers. No interface is DOWN or missing, no duplicate MACs exist, no ACL rules are dropping traffic, and no traffic-control shaping is applied to any interface.

**Localization:** No device or interface is implicated. All four topology devices (pc1, pc2, router1, router2) are clean across every diagnostic layer.

**Root cause:** There is no active fault — the reported connectivity loss cannot be reproduced or traced to any current mechanism in the lab.

**Fix:** No corrective action is needed. The NOC's recommendation to bounce the WAN interface or restart a device is unwarranted. If the user experienced a transient disruption, it has self-resolved. The incident should be monitored for recurrence; if it repeats, run `bgp_snapshot` and check router BGP logs at the time of the event.
